{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROUND 3: BLOCK, one finding, ACCEPTED — and checking the convention showed BOTH SIDES OF MY CONTRADICTION WERE WRONG.\n\nTHE FINDING: Approach said 'a migration window of one minor release' while Scope said the deprecation is registered with remove_in=_MAJOR (v1.0.0). Two different windows in one plan. I introduced the second half myself in round 1 while fixing a different finding — remediating one criterion and creating an inconsistency with another.\n\nWHAT THE CONVENTION ACTUALLY IS, checked rather than chosen: ALL SIX existing env-var deprecations in _deprecations.REGISTRY use _permanent(...), NOT _scheduled(...) — REBAR_NO_SYNC, COMPACT_THRESHOLD, SCRATCH_BASE_DIR, REBAR_ACLI_TIMEOUT, RECONCILER_ABSENT_GET_BUDGET, REBAR_ID_GUARD_MODE. _permanent sets remove_in=None with permanent=True: it warns and NEVER removes. So this repo's practice for a deprecated env var is to keep the old name working indefinitely and point at the replacement.\n\nNeither 'one minor release' nor 'remove at v1.0.0' matched that. Had I simply picked a side of my own contradiction I would have shipped a plan that deviates from six precedents — and removing a documented env var is a breaking change, made worse here because this one is named on the project README. Adopted _permanent.\n\nA SECOND PROBLEM DISSOLVED AS A RESULT, which is why the convention check was worth more than the fix: the S8 kill-switch DURABILITY concern from round 2 is now moot. I had argued that S8's runbook naming REBAR_LLM_MODEL would rot when the variable was removed at the next major. Under a permanent alias it is never removed, so the runbook never rots, and expressing the kill switch in class terms drops from 'needed for durability' to 'preferable for coherence'. The eb6e note has been corrected accordingly — I would rather withdraw a concern than leave a stale argument standing on another ticket.\n\nRound-by-round on this ticket: (1) _deprecations.py missing from Scope + a MEASURED enumeration that missed README.md; (2) fan-out placed in the wrong module + the S8 cross-ticket conflict; (3) a window contradiction I introduced in round 1. Every finding legitimate, and rounds 1 and 3 were both self-inflicted while fixing something else — the cost of editing a plan under review pressure rather than re-reading the whole thing after each change."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785586005248259001,"uuid":"28a78486-3f0b-4e60-a949-9cdd3989c7c0"}